  ],
  "text": "Count the spirals on a sunflower and you will land on thirty four, or fifty five, or eighty nine. Those are not random. Each one is the sum of the two numbers before it. The plant is not counting anything. It places each new seed a fixed turn from the last. That turn is about one hundred and thirty seven and a half degrees. And that specific angle is the only one that never lines up with itself. Any simpler fraction of a circle and the seeds fall into rows, leaving gaps. This angle keeps every new seed landing in the largest space left. Same reason pinecones spiral, and leaves stagger up a stem instead of shading each other. But be careful with the rest of it. The golden ratio in art and faces is mostly retrofitted. The plant maths is completely real. The Parthenon claim mostly is not. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
